.TH RAMPTRIAL 1
.SH NAME
ramptrial, stalerate \- error rate against link budget, and how often a frame is abandoned
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/radio/ramptrial.py
.I sat-session
.I gnd-session
.RI [ runs
.RI [ ramps... ]]
.br
.B tools/radio/stalerate.py
.I answerer-session
.I caller-session
.RI [ runs ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Ramptrial
sweeps the transmitter's power and measures what the link does.
.B "PA_CFG2's PA_POWER_RAMP is a step attenuator already inside the part"
\-
.CW "(ramp+1)/2 - 18"
dBm, 31dB in half-dB steps \- with no connectors, no pigtails and none
of the leakage that floors a cabled pad between two boards a metre
apart.  Both boards get the same ramp, because a one-sided change makes
the two directions different links and the counters cannot be compared.
.PP
.B "What it is NOT is the same experiment as attenuating the path."
Attenuating the path moves the receiver's operating point only;
attenuating the transmitter also changes what its own carrier sense
hears and where the far AGC settles.  The curve is the link budget
either way and the difference matters before it is quoted as a receiver
sensitivity.
.PP
.B Stalerate
flashes nothing and changes nothing: it runs
.IR twostyx (1)
.I runs
times and tallies
.B rxstale
and
.BR crcbad .
It exists because a fault that appears one or twice a run cannot be
called absent from three clean runs, and because an instrument added to
an interrupt path can perturb the timing it measures \- two
explanations that only a rate can separate.
.SH "WHAT RAMPTRIAL FOUND"
.DS
.CW "ramp  dBm    bigread  stale  crcbad  last"
.CW "  15  -10.0  1 of 1   1      1       -17"
.CW "  11  -12.0  1 of 1   2      2       -18"
.CW "   7  -14.0  1 of 1   1      1       -20"
.CW "   3  -16.0  1 of 1   0      2       -22"
.CW "   1  -17.0  1 of 1   0      2       -54"
.CW "   0  -17.5  1 of 1   0      2       -86"
.DE
.B "The PA is violently non-linear at the bottom."
The formula holds from 15 down to 3 \- about a decibel out over six \-
and then ramp 3 to ramp 0, 1.5dB by the formula, is
.B 64dB
at the receiver.  The cliff is useful as one large step and
.B "cannot be interpolated;"
anything quoting those bottom two rows as dBm is quoting the formula
and not the part.
.PP
.B "And the link survived all 69dB."
Six bigreads of six, errors flat or better as the signal fell.  So the
frames being abandoned are not lost because the signal is weak, which
is the useful half:
.B "a step attenuator would not have found this either."
.SH "THREE WAYS THIS REPORTED NOTHING FIRST"
Each is in the source as a comment, because each would have been
published as a result.
.IP \(bu
.B "Nothing read txramp."
.I /lib/radio
documented the key and only
.B armv7max25.b
ever read it, so a station's
.B txramp=
line did nothing.  The tell was identical counters AND an identical RSSI
at two powers 2dB apart.  Both stations print the ramp they applied now
and a row that cannot show both says
.BR "NOT APPLIED" .
.IP \(bu
.B "The counter parse glued the end prefix onto each field."
.CW "\(dq\e| \(dq + \(dqrxstale (\ed+)\(dq"
demands the pipe immediately before the field, and the line reads
.CW "| rxstall 2 rxpartial 3 resume 0 rxstale 2" ,
so every error column silently returned its default.
.IP \(bu
.B "And then it kept every line that was not the far end,"
which includes the other station's own lines.  With one console dead it
was the wrong board every time, identical at six power levels, and that
read as a flat curve.  A missing console is an error now.
.SH SOURCE
.B tools/radio/ramptrial.py
.br
.B tools/radio/stalerate.py
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR roleswap (1),
.IR radiocal (1),
.IR radio (3),
.B doc/radio-plan.ms
.SH BUGS
About six minutes a point, nearly all of it flashing, and it reflashes
when only
.I /lib/radio
changed because the root is inside the image.
